#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90216c0e-e4a5-5933-86e0-58d218939e29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2662e2-28f0-51b7-9fa7-ba07f2de0f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570956} {:answer/id #uuid "40996b84-32b7-51ec-905b-ddfdf12f19b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570987} {:answer/id #uuid "56e179c3-86b2-5a02-b674-d9a1a939689c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571024} {:answer/id #uuid "96927bba-3ed8-5280-a942-2dfccd2e993b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571129} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6146d2-2330-51c3-80dd-b7396e3f5dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56e179c3-86b2-5a02-b674-d9a1a939689c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571024} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e19b73d-cabe-517c-9e65-72cddd518722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f76581a2-daa4-550c-b579-94540f5aa5ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-63977" :question/problem "What is the index of the term $96$ for the geometric sequence below?\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=4{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=6,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This geometric sequence is given as a recurrence relation, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=6,\\,r=4.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in finding $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=96.$ Using the formula for the $n$th term, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 96 & =6\\cdot {4}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{96}{6} & ={4}^{n-1} \\\\ 16 & ={4}^{n-1} \\\\ {4}^{2} & ={4}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n2=n-1\\,⟹\\,n=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, $96$ is the $3$rd term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90216c0e-e4a5-5933-86e0-58d218939e29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aae7201-0fed-53fc-a560-be8854d1fa86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570930} {:answer/id #uuid "619a7f6a-6521-5fab-8631-a66510798252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571041} {:answer/id #uuid "688648e4-8fd7-5010-8190-cf083062eac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571053} {:answer/id #uuid "9306cacc-8d60-5b87-8b47-e67068d937e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571120} {:answer/id #uuid "c7619aae-7b10-5fbe-b77e-14e4e7134260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aae7201-0fed-53fc-a560-be8854d1fa86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570930} :answer-field/id #uuid "00ed5582-4e3e-5fcb-9fc3-a83454376310" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96ea02ed-6d88-5b00-815c-f8be25c4ea42" :question/math-academy-id "q-63978" :question/problem "What is the index of the term $640$ for the geometric sequence below?\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=4f(n),\\,f(1)=10,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This geometric sequence is given as a recurrence relation, i.e.,\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n).\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\nf(1)=10,\\,r=4.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in finding $n$ such that $f(n)=640.$ Using the formula for the $n$th term, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 640 & =10\\cdot {4}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{640}{10} & ={4}^{n-1} \\\\ 64 & ={4}^{n-1} \\\\ {4}^{3} & ={4}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n3=n-1\\,⟹\\,n=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $640$ is the $4$th term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078565 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa87d67-d9df-5bbb-b98e-00d72d16fe7f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Particular Term When the First Term is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bd4e7d8-50eb-5592-9e14-57d4fe27d0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570957} {:answer/id #uuid "55180673-cfac-5260-9318-f71da80eff56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571019} {:answer/id #uuid "8a8e13ad-2f9f-5475-93f3-c68d44d1735f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571104} {:answer/id #uuid "b70adf20-ddef-58f0-b112-4b95fcd85d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571173} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c9e500-94c8-5652-86b9-f435bc1248ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2c9e500-94c8-5652-86b9-f435bc1248ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571214} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c632047-5477-54e3-8a2f-d791ae58065c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f336a0d-d13a-5e94-83e6-97790680d0c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-67482" :question/problem "Given that a geometric sequence has the $4$th term ${a}_{4}=250$ and the common ratio $r=5$, what is the index of the term $1\\,250?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=250$, we can use the formula for the $n$th term to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{4-1} \\\\ 250 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{4-1} \\\\ 250 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{250}{125} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} &=2\\cdot {5}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=1\\,250,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,250 & =2\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{1\\,250}{2} & ={5}^{n-1} \\\\ 625 & ={5}^{n-1} \\\\ {5}^{4} & ={5}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n4=n-1\\,⟹\\,n=5\n$$"}]}
